i i love racing i love training as well  which i think is a huge bonus  and and i have a an incredibly  supportive family behind me and two kids  who absolutely love being at races as  well  so i think um yeah i'm very fortunate  but i've also it's incredibly hard  and i know obviously you would have been  watching the olympics how have you found  it watching it in tokyo  well that's one of those strange things  about preparing for three events at the  paralympics is you only get  a small amount of a snapshot and you  focus on events that are potentially  going to help you with your preparations  so  i was immersed in the road events i've  followed as many track events as i can  but i haven't been able to watch it  wall-to-wall as perhaps i would have  liked to and i would have done as a  child  and so i'm very excited to get out to  japan and to see it all for myself  obviously this game has been set up you  know  with a backdrop of very unusual  circumstances we have covert 19 to  contend with here  do you have any worries at all any  concerns at all going to tokyo with that  i think um as craig spence said um and  as was said  uh in the report before you know there's  a bubble being created  for the athletes and the coaching staff  and anybody who's within  you know a touching distance of athletes  on a regular basis  and that bubble protects not just the  people within it but also the people  outside of it  and we're tested every day so i tested  yesterday and i'm now tested every day  right the way through to the day of  departure  and all of those tests will make sure  that if there is any infection it gets  picked up at the earliest opportunity  and we've been following some incredibly  strict rules ahead of going to the games  my whole family are back in quarantine  we have been for um just  over a week now and we're doing  everything we can before we leave to  make sure that nothing gets on the plane  with us  so there's a huge amount of commitment  not just from the athletes and the staff  going  but also from the families who are  supporting us to depart from the country  as well  now you first went to the paralympics in  1992 as a swimmer  you've since switched on to cycling  which we'll touch on very briefly later  on but  firstly i just want to know from your  point of view how has the games  changed over the years  well my first games in 92 was a the  games before any digital media  so you found out you were selected um  because a letter dropped on your doormat  i think people found out in the  newspaper on the news  on teletext there wasn't you know all  the digital platforms to find out team  news  and finding out information about the  games was very different as well  um and but the games was no less  inspiring or exciting and i remember  going to  barcelona and yeah i've not even got  started my gcse courses  and but it was just the most immense  festival of sports and that stayed with  the games all the way through  the past seven games that i've done and  will ultimately stay with us through  into the games in japan as well  celebration of the best athletes in the  world coming together  every four or five years as it is on  this occasion to just perform to the  very best of their ability  and to put all the hard work onto that  playing field onto that race performance  and to see you know who comes out on top  and just very quickly then how did you  make that transition  from um swimming to cycling  well i started by going down to the  manchester velodrome to ride on the  on the track in what they call a taster  session and i think you could do that in  any velodrome in the country we've got  five indoor velodromes  and a number of outdoor velodromes so  you go to a public session you learn to  ride on the track  um and then i um decided that i was  gonna start racing on the track  i became the first athlete within the  talent transfer program  so after racing successfully at the  european championships 